diff --git a/Assets/XML/Text/AdvCiv-SAS_Sevopedia_Lengthy.xml b/Assets/XML/Text/AdvCiv-SAS_Sevopedia_Lengthy.xml
index c84005984..1442633a0 100644
--- a/Assets/XML/Text/AdvCiv-SAS_Sevopedia_Lengthy.xml
+++ b/Assets/XML/Text/AdvCiv-SAS_Sevopedia_Lengthy.xml
@@ -656,7 +656,7 @@ Created as part of AdvCiv-SAS improvements
 
 
 
-	<!-- custom: replace "JAPANESE_SHALE_PLANT" with "JAPAN_DOUJOU"; as for the pedia entry, similarly imported from/based on https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dojo and modified or not or also transliterated or not as i wanted or not or yes or etc for advciv-sas -->
+	<!-- custom: replace "JAPANESE_SHALE_PLANT" with "JAPAN_DOUJOU"; as for the pedia entry, similarly based on https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dojo -->
 	<TEXT>
 		<Tag>TXT_KEY_BUILDING_JAPAN_DOUJOU_PEDIA</Tag>
 		<English>[TAB]A doujou (often transliterated as "dojo") is a hall or place for immersive learning, experiential learning, or meditation. This is traditionally in the field of martial arts. The term literally means "place of the Way" in Japanese.[PARAGRAPH:2]The word doujou originates from Buddhism. Initially, doujou were adjunct to temples and were formal training places for any of the Japanese arts ending in "-dou", from the Chinese Dao, meaning "way" or "path". Sometimes meditation halls where Zen Buddhists practice zazen meditation were called doujou. The alternative term zen-do is more specific, and more widely used. European Soutou Zen groups affiliated with the International Zen Association prefer to use doujou instead of zendo to describe their meditation halls as did their founding master, Taisen Deshimaru.[PARAGRAPH:1]In Japan, any facility for physical training, including professional wrestling, may be called a doujou. In the Western world, the term doujou (when related to physical activity) is used exclusively for Japanese martial arts such as aikidou, juudou, karate-dou, etc.[PARAGRAPH:2]A proper Japanese martial arts doujou is considered special and is well cared for by its users. Shoes are not worn in a doujou, which is in line with the Japanese custom to keep the premises clean and respectable. In many styles it is traditional to conduct a ritual cleaning (souji) of the doujou at the beginning or end of each training session. Besides the obvious hygienic benefits of regular cleaning it also serves to reinforce the fact that doujou are supposed to be supported and managed by the student body (or by special students, e.g., uchi-deshi). In some cases, the instructional staff may choose to help the students clean, as a sign of modesty, setting a personal example. In Modern Times, even when a school has a Dojo to train inside of, the training may sometimes be conducted outside, often in parks.[PARAGRAPH:1]Many traditional doujou follow a prescribed pattern with shomen ("front") and various entrances that are used based on student and instructor rank laid out precisely. The Shomen is designated as a wall, usually in the North of the Dojo and facing the entrance. Typically students will enter in the lower-left corner of the doujou (in reference to the shomen) with instructors in the upper right corner. Shomen typically has a shelf attached to it. On the shelf would traditionally be presented a miniature Shintou shrine with a sculpture, flower arrangement, or other artifacts. Pictures of past Masters are generally found aside the shrine, and not on it. The term kamiza means "place of honor" and a related term, kamidana refers to the shrine itself. Other artifacts may be displayed throughout the doujou, such as kanban (signboard) that authorize the school in a style or strategy, and items such as taiko drums or armor (ou-yoroi). It is not uncommon to find the name of the doujou and the doujou kun (roughly "doujou rules") displayed prominently at shomen as well. Visitors may have a special place reserved, depending on their rank and station. Weapons and other training gear will normally be found on the back wall.[PARAGRAPH:2]The term doujou is also increasingly used for other forms of immersive-learning space.[PARAGRAPH:1]The term doujou is sometimes used to describe the meditation halls where Zen Buddhists practice zazen seated meditation. The alternative term zen-do is more specific, and more widely used. European Soutou Zen groups affiliated with the International Zen Association prefer to use doujou instead of zendo to describe their meditation halls as did their founding master, Taisen Deshimaru.[PARAGRAPH:1]The term 'Dojo' has been used for various software, digital learning applications and by related companies.</English>

